+
+<h1>Image delivery for low-cost phones</h1>
+<p class="sub">A system-design walkthrough of the <code>fastimages</code> feed — ComposeLearning</p>
+<p><span class="tag">Jetpack Compose</span> <span class="tag">Coil 3</span> <span class="tag">OkHttp 5</span> <span class="tag">India-first constraints</span></p>
+
+<div class="thesis">
+<strong>The thesis.</strong> Showing an image is a UI problem. Showing it fast on a ₹9,000 phone
+on a congested cell is a systems problem, and the answer is not "download a smaller image" —
+it is a <em>decision function</em> that runs on the device, resolves capability into a small
+set of variant parameters, and keeps every cache layer between the phone and the blob store
+shareable. Screen density is the input everyone reaches for first and it is the one that
+misleads you most.
+</div>
+
+<h2>1. The problem and the constraints</h2>
+<p>
+A home-screen grid of ~120 food photos. The user opens the app, sees tiles immediately, and
+flings. What "good" means, in priority order:
+</p>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Something is on screen in the first frame.</strong> Never blank, never white, never jumping.</li>
+  <li><strong>Scroll never drops a frame</strong>, including while images arrive and decode.</li>
+  <li><strong>Bytes are proportionate</strong> to what the user can actually see and afford.</li>
+  <li><strong>Degradation is graceful</strong>: slow link, cheap device, Data Saver on, network lost mid-scroll.</li>
+</ol>
+<p>The constraints that make it interesting:</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Budget phone: 1080×2400 screen, 2–3 GB RAM, 8 slow cores, ~96 MB heap</td>
+      <td>A high-density screen on a device that cannot afford high-density images. <strong>Decode CPU and bitmap memory bind before bandwidth does.</strong> One 1080×1080 ARGB_8888 bitmap is 4.4 MB of heap.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Congested cellular: 300–800 kbit/s, 200–600 ms RTT</td>
+      <td>Round trips hurt more than bytes. Anything needing a second request per tile is suspect.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Metered data, Data Saver commonly enabled</td>
+      <td>Speculative work is spending someone else's money. It needs an off switch driven by user intent.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Tens of thousands of Android SKUs</td>
+      <td>Per-device responses are unshareable. Cache keys must describe <em>bytes</em>, not devices.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2>2. Architecture</h2>
+
+<figure>
