+  <rect x="2" y="222" width="710" height="70" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#c9d1dc"/>
+  <text x="14" y="240" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1f2430">One cache key, four layers</text>
+  <text x="14" y="256" font-size="9" fill="#5b6472">seed@320x240#webp-q62 &#8594; L1 · L2 · L3 · L4 all keyed on the same derived variant, never on the device.</text>
+  <text x="14" y="272" font-size="9" fill="#5b6472">9 buckets × 4 qualities × 2 formats = 72 shareable derivatives for the entire device population.</text>
+  <text x="14" y="286" font-size="9" fill="#a83a3a">A prefetch that warms a different key than the tile later requests has a 100% miss rate and still spends the bandwidth.</text>
+</svg>
+<figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Four cache layers, one key. The client composes the URL; every layer downstream is shared.</figcaption>
+</figure>
+
+<div class="warn">
+<strong>The question interviewers push on:</strong> "why not send the device profile to the server and
+let it decide?" Because the response would then vary per device model. You would need
+<code>Vary</code> headers, and the CDN edge — the layer doing the most work — would fragment across
+tens of thousands of SKUs. Resolving locally turns that population into ~72 shared objects.
+Server-side device detection via User-Agent is a web-era pattern and its cost is exactly this.
+</div>
+
+<h2>3. The decision function</h2>
+<p>Everything reduces to one expression, evaluated on the device:</p>
+<p style="text-align:center;font-family:'SF Mono',Menlo,monospace;font-size:10pt;background:#f1f3f6;padding:8px;border-radius:4px">
+variant = f(layout size, density, device tier, network tier, user intent)
+</p>
+
