+<h3>Why measure throughput yourself</h3>
+<p>
+<code>NetworkCapabilities.getLinkDownstreamBandwidthKbps()</code> is a radio <em>capability hint</em>,
+not a measurement — it will report "LTE, 20 Mbps" on a congested cell delivering 300 kbit/s.
+So throughput comes from <code>okhttp3.EventListener.responseBodyStart/End</code>, which brackets
+<strong>body bytes only</strong>. DNS, TCP, TLS and time-to-first-byte are latency; folding them
+into a bandwidth figure makes a fast link to a distant server look slow.
+</p>
+
+<div class="warn">
+<strong>Tier flapping is a real bug, not a theoretical one.</strong> The tier feeds the URL, so every
+flip changes the cache key and re-downloads the visible screen. Four defences: re-classify on a
+5 s timer rather than per sample; require a 25% margin to move <em>up</em> a tier; enforce a 10 s
+minimum dwell; and publish a rounded kbit/s so the policy never sees jitter that only the debug
+panel should see.
+</div>
