+<h2>7. What is done to make scrolling smooth — the cancellation story</h2>
+<p>
+Smoothness is mostly about <em>work you refuse to do</em>. Four mechanisms, at four scopes:
+</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Scope</th><th>Trigger</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Observable as</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>One tile</td><td>tile scrolls out of composition</td><td><code>AsyncImagePainter</code> is a <code>RememberObserver</code>; <code>onForgotten</code> disposes the request</td><td><code>cancelled</code> counter</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Prefetch window</td><td>direction change / new window</td><td><code>submit()</code> cancels the previous <code>Job</code>; children die with it</td><td>in-flight count drops</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Fling</td><td>40 windows crossed in 400 ms</td><td>90 ms settle delay + <code>collectLatest</code></td><td>only one window is ever queued</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Screen</td><td>navigating away</td><td><code>rememberCoroutineScope</code> cancels; loader and caches survive</td><td>no orphaned work, warm return visit</td></tr>
+</table>
+<div class="note">
+<strong>The counter-intuitive metric:</strong> a healthy fling <em>should</em> produce cancellations. Zero
+cancellations during fast scrolling means requests are outliving their views — which is both
+wasted bandwidth and a decode competing with the frame you are trying to draw. The panel
+surfaces <code>cancelled</code> for exactly that reason.
+</div>
+<p>
+The other half of smoothness is bounded concurrency. Prefetches and visible cells share one
+OkHttp dispatcher, and HTTP/2 stream priority is not something OkHttp exposes usefully — so
+priority is enforced structurally instead: 3 parallel eager prefetches, 2 disk-only, nearest
+first, and the window shrinks on slow links and low-tier devices.
+</p>
+
+<h2>8. What is done to track failures</h2>
+<p>
+Instrumentation lives in a Coil <code>EventListener</code> plus an OkHttp <code>EventListener</code>, writing to
+plain atomics that the UI polls. Nothing is pushed into snapshot state.
+</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Source</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>bytes on the wire</td><td>OkHttp <code>responseBodyEnd(call, byteCount)</code></td><td>Exact, unlike <code>Content-Length</code>, which is absent under chunked encoding and lies under compression. Cache hits never reach it.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>memory / disk / network split</td><td>Coil <code>SuccessResult.dataSource</code></td><td>The headline number: share of displayed images that never touched the network</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>fetch ms, decode ms</td><td><code>fetchStart/End</code>, <code>decodeStart/End</code></td><td>Separates a bandwidth problem from a CPU problem — the distinction that decides what to fix on a low-tier device</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>cancelled</td><td><code>onCancel</code></td><td>Scroll hygiene (see above)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>downgrades</td><td>timeout fallback path</td><td>How often the aggressive timeout is firing — i.e. whether the budget is calibrated</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>failed</td><td><code>onError</code></td><td>Terminal failures after the ladder bottoms out</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>prefetch completions</td><td><code>Extras.Key</code> tag on the request</td><td>Speculative work kept out of the display statistics — an untagged prefetch inflates your hit rate and hides the truth</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>bitmap cache occupancy</td><td><code>MemoryCache.size / maxSize</code></td><td>Whether the budget is the binding constraint</td></tr>
+</table>
+<p><strong>In production, every one of these is segmented by device tier × network tier.</strong> A global
+p50 hides the users this entire design exists for. The SLIs worth alerting on:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>image p95 latency, per segment — not a global average</li>
+  <li>cache hit rate per layer: L1 memory, L2 disk, L3 CDN edge</li>
+  <li>bytes per session, and <em>prefetch waste rate</em> (prefetched but never shown)</li>
+  <li>time to first meaningful image on the home screen</li>
+  <li>timeout rate and downgrade rate — a rising downgrade rate means the timeout ladder needs remote-config surgery, not a code change</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>9. Trade-offs worth raising unprompted</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Choice</th><th>The tension</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>AVIF vs WebP</strong></td><td>AVIF is 20–30% smaller <em>and</em> materially slower to decode on weak CPUs. On a low-tier device it can cost more in decode than it saves in transfer. "Fewer bytes" and "faster" are not the same thing. Gate it on tier and A/B test — do not enable it globally because a blog post said it is smaller.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>HARDWARE vs RGB_565</strong></td><td>Mutually exclusive. Hardware bitmaps keep pixels out of the Java heap but cost ~1 FD each and forbid pixel access; RGB_565 halves heap but is software and wrong wherever alpha exists. Many teams keep hardware everywhere and cap the <em>bucket</em> instead — the lever that cuts transfer and decode at once.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>BitmapPool / <code>inBitmap</code></strong></td><td class="no">Do not add this. Correct advice in the Glide/Fresco era; obsolete now. ART's generational GC made short-lived bitmap allocation cheap, <code>inBitmap</code> requires software bitmaps (so a pool forces <code>allowHardware(false)</code> — trading the big win for a small one), and hardware bitmaps cannot be pooled at all because their pixels live in graphics memory. Coil removed its pool deliberately.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Bucket granularity</strong></td><td>Fewer buckets means a hotter edge cache and a smaller derivative cache, but more over-fetch per device. More buckets is the reverse. It is a cache-efficiency vs byte-efficiency dial, and it belongs in remote config.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Prefetch aggressiveness</strong></td><td>Every prefetch is a bet placed with the user's data allowance. Track the waste rate; if it is high you are buying latency with someone else's money.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Quality-tiered URLs</strong></td><td>Making quality part of the URL means a network change changes the cache key. That is why the never-downgrade ledger exists — without it, adaptivity actively harms the user who just walked out of WiFi.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Separate LQIP request</strong></td><td>Rejected. On a 400 ms-RTT link, a second request per tile doubles round trips before anything is visible. A hash that rides in the catalog JSON is close to free.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2>10. Implementation map</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>File</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>DevicePolicy.kt</code></td><td>Hardware signals → <code>LOW/MID/HIGH</code> → cache budget, bucket ceiling, bitmap config, AVIF eligibility. Static for the process.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>NetworkMonitor.kt</code></td><td>Transport, metered, Data Saver, and a measured EWMA throughput → tier, with dwell time and upgrade margin to stop flapping.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>DeliveryPolicy.kt</code></td><td>The decision function. <code>DeliveryProfile</code> + the downgrade ladder + the volatile handoff to the network layer.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>ImageCdn.kt</code></td><td>URL and cache-key construction: width ladder, quality, format, and the naive master for comparison.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>VariantResolver.kt</code></td><td>The single producer of variants. Shared by tiles and prefetcher; owns the never-downgrade ledger.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>FeedImageEngine.kt</code></td><td>Tuned <code>ImageLoader</code>, OkHttp dispatcher/pool, transport measurement listener, adaptive per-call timeout interceptor, <code>@Immutable</code> holder.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>ImagePrefetcher.kt</code></td><td>Two-tier, direction-biased, cancellable prefetch with bounded parallelism.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>ImageLoadMetrics.kt</code></td><td>Pipeline instrumentation as atomics; prefetches tagged out of the display stats.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>MicroThumb.kt</code></td><td>Blur-hash placeholder that ships in the catalog payload. BlurHash/ThumbHash stand-in.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>FeedModels.kt</code></td><td>The catalog shape: ids and metadata, <strong>never finished URLs</strong>. Aspect ratio bucketed to two crops.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>FastImageFeedScreen.kt</code></td><td>Grid, tiles, timeout-fallback state, metrics panel.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>DeliveryPolicyPanel.kt</code></td><td>Makes the decision visible and forceable — the only practical way to test the low-end path.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2>11. Honest scorecard</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Production concern</th><th>In the sample</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Bucketed WebP variants, per-surface sizing</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Device tiering → cache budget, bucket cap, bitmap config</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Network tiering with measured throughput + anti-flap</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Data Saver / lite mode honoured</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Blur-hash placeholder in the payload + payload aspect ratio</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Two-tier direction-biased prefetch, decode-free far tier</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Shared cache keys; never downgrade a decoded image</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Adaptive per-call timeouts → downgrade ladder</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Cancel on detachment, and instrumented</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>In-app metrics for bytes, cache split, fetch/decode split</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Remote config over every ladder and cut-off</td><td class="no">no — the one thing you must not ship as constants</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Telemetry segmented by device × network tier</td><td class="no">no</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Real BlurHash/ThumbHash with a server-side encoder</td><td class="no">no — synthesised locally, since picsum cannot describe its own photos</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Progressive JPEG</td><td class="no">no — and <code>BitmapFactory</code> gives no incremental display for free</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Paging 3, <code>PersistentList</code>, baseline profile</td><td class="no">no</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2>12. If you get 60 seconds to answer</h2>
+<p>
+"We never send the master image and we never send device info. The client measures its cell
+width, classifies the device from RAM and heap and the network from measured throughput, and
+folds those into three URL parameters — width bucket, quality, format. Buckets keep the CDN edge
+and our disk cache shareable across the whole device population. A dominant colour and a
+30-byte blur hash ride in the catalog JSON so the first frame is never empty and the aspect ratio
+is known before any byte arrives. We prefetch two tiers ahead in the direction of travel —
+decoded for the next six, bytes-only for the twelve after that — and cancel the window on
+direction change. On a low-tier device the binding constraint is decode CPU and bitmap memory,
+not bandwidth, so the cache budget is a share of the heap and the prefetch window halves.
+Timeouts are per-call and adaptive; on expiry we ask for a smaller variant rather than retrying
+the same one. And we measure the cache split, the fetch/decode split and the cancellation rate,
+segmented by tier — because a global p50 hides exactly the users this design is for."
+</p>
+
